Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Does the Wharf Express Whistle Twice?
- Story & Context: At the Harumi Freight Yard during the sweltering heatwave of July 1975, recording engineer Kenjiro Mori set up his two-track tape recorder on the platform just as a diesel switcher engine jammed its brake shoe against the rail, releasing a screech that perfectly matched an incoming freighter's horn in Tokyo Bay. Mori handed a slide trombone to sixteen-year-old vocalist Chiyo Mori, who improvised an ecstatic sunny melody over the deafening mechanical noise while dockworkers slammed cargo wrenches against empty oil drums in perfect calypso time. The resulting live take captured the exact moment industrial noise became street music, punctuated by Chiyo screaming into the sea wind, "When the noon whistle blows, kiss me before the cargo sails away!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: 1970s vintage record sleeve illustration showing a canary-yellow freight train stalled on a wharf platform, framed by hibiscus flowers, palm fronds, and giant marine navigation horns blasting visible white soundwaves across a glittering turquoise harbor under a blazing sun.

Track 2: Tadao Missed the Salt-Train
- Story & Context: Running down the wooden steps of Tsurumi Station with a bruised lip and a dented brass trombone, young deckhand Tadao Sato could only watch the red tail-lights of the 06:10 departure dissolve into the dense harbor haze, leaving behind the bitter smell of creosote and burnt orange peel. Leaning over the platform railing, Tadao raised his horn and blown-blasted a lonely two-mile drone that rattled the station clock, answered instantly by the deep foghorn of a Panama-flagged container vessel creeping down the canal. His sweetheart stood stranded on the opposite track clutching a woven bamboo travel bag, listening to the dual horns synchronize as Tadao belted across the rails, "Leave the luggage behind, my heart is anchored on the loading dock!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Retro anime cel aesthetic from 1978, depicting a young Japanese trombonist in a soaked striped sailors shirt weeping on an empty wooden train station platform, warm tropical morning light piercing through thick harbor fog, pastel hues, cinematic lens flare.

Track 3: The Locomotive Swallows Fog
- Story & Context: Legendary dockside lore claims that the late-night freight locomotive connecting Kawasaki Pier to the mainland ran with its firebox open during the Great Typhoon of 1978, its steam exhaust blending seamlessly into the impenetrable sea mist until the Keihin Canal itself seemed to sing. Captain Ota of the patrol boat Chidori recorded the event on his shipboard radio, capturing the engine’s shrill steam whistle trading long, harmonic pulses with three anchored supertankers until the channel water literally fizzed with low-frequency resonance. Harumi Signal Club later overdubbed shimmering marimba runs and joyous four-part idol harmonies over Ota’s archival tape, giving birth to the mythical anthem where the chorus proclaims, "We swallowed the white mist and spat out pure golden morning light!"
